# ============================================================================
# Dynamic UI — root environment file, for `docker compose up`.
# Copy to `.env` (already .gitignore'd) and fill in real values. Every
# service reads this same file via `env_file: .env` in docker-compose.yml —
# a single root file rather than one per service, chosen for this project's
# size (see that file's top-of-file comment). Service-to-service URLs
# (ARTIFACTS_SERVER_URL, TOOL_SERVICE_URL, etc.) are NOT set here — they're
# set directly in docker-compose.yml, since they need compose's internal
# DNS (service names), which wouldn't work for anyone running a service
# outside Docker against this same file.
#
# For non-Docker local dev, each service still reads its own
# services/<name>/.env instead — see README.md.
# ============================================================================

# --- postgres ---
# Two options — pick one, no docker-compose.yml edits required either way:
#
# OPTION A (default): the bundled `postgres` service in docker-compose.yml.
# Just fill in these three vars; DATABASE_URL/DIRECT_URL are built from them
# automatically in docker-compose.yml.
POSTGRES_USER=dynamic_ui
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=dynamic_ui

# OPTION B: an external Postgres (e.g. Supabase). Uncomment both lines below
# and fill in your real connection strings — Supabase gives you a pooled
# ("Transaction" mode, port 6543) URL for DATABASE_URL and a direct
# ("Session" mode, port 5432) URL for DIRECT_URL (Prisma needs the direct
# one for migrations). Once these are set, docker-compose.yml's tool-service
# uses them instead of the bundled postgres container automatically (see
# that file's comments) — the POSTGRES_* vars above are then unused, and you
# can optionally comment out the `postgres` service in docker-compose.yml
# too, so it doesn't start for nothing.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.xxxxx:<password>@aws-0-xxxxx.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
# DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.xxxxx:<password>@aws-0-xxxxx.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# --- tool-service ---
# Signs and verifies every access token login/register mint. Held only by
# tool-service — every other service asks it to verify a token rather than
# decoding one itself.
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# --- artifact-agent-service ---
# opencode's own Anthropic credential, read directly from the process
# environment at invocation (verified at container startup — see
# services/artifact-agent-service/docker-entrypoint.sh). This service's own
# application code never reads this key itself.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-key-here
# OPENCODE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900
#
# NOTE — audit finding, not silently fixed: there is intentionally no
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL (or LLM_PROVIDER/GEMINI_*) entry here. Provider/model
# switching was removed from this service; opencode always runs with a
# fixed model, OPENCODE_MODEL, a constant in
# services/artifact-agent-service/src/config.ts (currently
# 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-5') — not an env var at all. Both
# services/artifact-agent-service/.env.example and src/config.ts already
# reflect this correctly (no leftover GEMINI_API_KEY/GEMINI_MODEL/
# LLM_PROVIDER references were found in either). The one piece of actual
# drift found during this pass is in the LOCAL, gitignored
# services/artifact-agent-service/.env file (never committed), which still
# sets an ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-3-5-sonnet value — config.ts confirms this
# is dead, silently-ignored config, not something this Docker/compose setup
# needs to account for. Fixing that local file's stale key (and rotating
# the real-looking Anthropic API key that same file has checked out on
# disk) is a manual follow-up for whoever owns that machine, left out of
# scope here per the instructions for this pass.

# --- db-agent-service ---
# Reuses ANTHROPIC_API_KEY above — this service calls Anthropic directly
# (no opencode subprocess involved).
DB_AGENT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# DB_AGENT_MAX_TOOL_ITERATIONS=6
# DB_AGENT_TOOL_CATALOG_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300

# --- artifacts-viewer (unified chat router) ---
# Reuses ANTHROPIC_API_KEY above. /api/chat classifies each message as an
# artifact-generation or database request before routing it (see
# apps/artifacts-viewer/src/lib/unified-chat/agent-router.ts) — a cheap
# classification call, so it defaults to Haiku rather than a frontier model.
AGENT_ROUTER_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# --- artifacts-viewer's own database (chat session history) ---
# Deliberately a SEPARATE Postgres database from tool-service's ERP database
# above (DATABASE_URL/DIRECT_URL) — not a second schema bolted onto it.
# artifacts-viewer is the only service that ever holds this credential; see
# apps/artifacts-viewer/prisma/schema.prisma's top-of-file note for why the
# two must never be joined or queried together in application code.
#
# OPTION A (default): the bundled `viewer-postgres` service in
# docker-compose.yml. Just fill in these three vars; VIEWER_DATABASE_URL is
# built from them automatically in docker-compose.yml.
VIEWER_POSTGRES_USER=artifacts_viewer
VIEWER_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-too
VIEWER_POSTGRES_DB=artifacts_viewer

# OPTION B: an external Postgres (e.g. a second/separate Supabase project —
# NOT the same project as DATABASE_URL above). Set VIEWER_DATABASE_URL
# directly instead; once set, docker-compose.yml's artifacts-viewer service
# uses it automatically (the VIEWER_POSTGRES_* vars above become unused —
# you can also comment out the `viewer-postgres` service in
# docker-compose.yml so it doesn't start for nothing).
# VIEWER_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.yyyyy:<password>@aws-0-yyyyy.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
